          "html": "Asked before booking",
          "align": "left"
         }
        },
        {
         "id": "b_96p2x1w6",
         "type": "accordion",
         "props": {
          "style": "separated",
          "single": true,
          "openFirst": false,
          "textColour": "",
          "items": [
           {
            "title": "Do I need sea legs?",
            "body": "Mostly no. Island hopping keeps to sheltered water by design, and the longer crossings happen at lunch, when a full table is the best stabiliser yet invented. If a passage looks lumpy the master will say so at breakfast and route round the worst of it."
           },
           {
            "title": "How much time is ashore?",
            "body": "Most days, four to six hours. The point of a small ship is that the gangway goes down where the town is, so an hour ashore is an hour ashore, not forty minutes of tender queue."
           },
           {
            "title": "What about luggage?",
            "body": "No limit worth printing. If you can carry it up a gangway, it sails. Kilts, cellos and one memorable spinning wheel have all made the trip."
           },
           {
            "title": "Is there wifi?",
            "body": "In the saloon, when the mast can see a shore it likes. It is honest wifi: good enough for photographs home, poor enough to save you from your inbox."
           }
          ]
         }
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
